<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yemelyan Pugachev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1742–<a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yemelyan Pugachev was a Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> who in 1773 claimed to be the murdered Emperor Peter III and led the largest Cossack and peasant rising in Russian history against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. Backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Yaik</a> Cossacks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>, factory serfs and peasants, his forces burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and swept through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region. Defeated in 1774, he was betrayed by his own followers and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in January 1775.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yemelyan-pugachev.jpg" alt="Yemelyan Pugachev" width="299" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yemelyan_Pugachev_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
